ine, and Surgery, Washington University School of Medicine, St Louis, Missouri 63110, USA."}]}}, {"fullName": "Monneret G", "lastName": "Monneret", "firstName": "Guillaume", "initials": "G", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-9961-5739"}}, {"fullName": "Payen D", "lastName": "Payen", "firstName": "Didier", "initials": "D"}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-9961-5739"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "12", "volume": "13", "journalIssueId": 2097645, "dateOfPublication": "2013 Dec", "monthOfPublication": 12, "yearOfPublication": 2013, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Nat Rev Immunol", "title": "Nature reviews. Immunology", "essn": "1474-1741", "issn": "1474-1733", "nlmid": "101124169", "isoabbreviation": "Nat Rev Immunol"}, "printPublicationDate": "2013-12-01"}, "pubYear": "2013", "pageInfo": "862-874", "abstractText": "Sepsis - which is a severe life-threatening infection with organ dysfunction - initiates a complex interplay of host pro-inflammatory and anti-inflammatory processes. Sepsis can be considered a race to the death between the pathogens and the host immune system, and it is the proper balance between the often competing pro- and anti-inflammatory pathways that determines the fate of the individual. Although the field of sepsis research has witnessed the failure of many highly touted clinical trials, a better understanding of the pathophysiological basis of the disorder and the mechanisms responsible for the associated pro- and anti-inflammatory responses provides a novel approach for treating this highly lethal condition. Biomarker-guided immunotherapy that is administered to patients at the proper immune phase of sepsis is potentially a major advance in the treatment of sepsis and in the field of infectious disease.", "affiliation": "Department of Anesthesiology, Medicine, and Surgery, Washington University School of Medicine, St Louis, Missouri 63110, USA.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't", "research-article", "Review", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NIGMS NIH HHS", "acronym": "GM", "grantId": "R01 GM098391", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIGMS NIH HHS", "acronym": "GM", "grantId": "GM44118", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIGMS NIH HHS", "acronym": "GM", "grantId": "R37 GM044118", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NCI NIH HHS", "acronym": "CA", "grantId": "P30 CA091842", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIGMS NIH HHS", "acronym": "GM", "grantId": "GM55194", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIGMS NIH HHS", "acronym": "GM", "grantId": "R01 GM055194", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIGMS NIH HHS", "acronym": "GM", "grantId": "R01 GM044118", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Animals"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Mice"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Sepsis", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "IM", "qualifierName": "immunology"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "TH", "qualifierName": "therapy"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Inflammation", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "IM", "qualifierName": "immunology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Immunotherapy"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Models, Animal"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Immune Tolerance"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Immunity, Cellular", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "IM", "qualifierName": "immunology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Immunity, Innate"}]}, "subsetList": {"subset": [{"code": "IM", "name": "Index Medicus"}]}, "fullTextUrlList":